{
  "question": "Is per-fish energy in survival_5k something behaviour can move, or is it pinned by the world config?",
  "finding": "Across a 4.5x change in food supply (auto_food_spawn_rate 9 -> 2) total fish energy moves 12% at baseline while max_population holds the count at 60, so per-fish energy is a constant of the configuration. Below the stock rate the controller saturates at its 3x boost ceiling and stops regulating: rates 18 and 36 show negative drift, meaning the tank is still draining when the run ends rather than sitting at a lower set point.",
  "reproduce": "python tools/measure_tank_regulation.py benchmarks/tank/survival_5k.py --key auto_food_spawn_rate --values 2,3,9,18,36 --seeds 42",
  "reads": {
    "raw_energy": "sum(fish.energy) - the quantity the food controller reads",
    "banked_energy": "sum(overflow_energy_bank) - invisible to the controller, and counted by the survival_5k score",
    "drift": "fractional change in raw_energy between the first and second half of the averaging window; near zero means a real steady state"
  },
  "incidental": [
    "The population arm of the controller is dead code in every tank benchmark: AUTO_FOOD_HIGH_POP_THRESHOLD_1 = 80 against caps of 50-60.",
    "survival_5k pins auto_food_spawn_rate: 9 against an engine default of 36, at which the benchmark is invalid (starvation 0.9528).",
    "After _draw_on_reserves the population holds at 60 even at the engine default, where baseline fell to 53.8."
  ],
